Description: Bound drum accompaniment generation in abc_add_drum()
 abc_add_drum() is built like abc_add_gchord() and carries the same defects,
 driven by "%%MIDI drum" instead of "%%MIDI gchord". No CVE is assigned for
 this one; it was found while fixing CVE-2026-108577 in the sibling function.
 .
  * stime = (tracktime - etime) * steps has no upper bound and the loop
    allocates an ABCEVENT per iteration via abc_add_drumnote()/
    abc_add_noteoff(), so a crafted file makes it allocate without bound.
    This is the same flaw as CVE-2026-108577.
  * h->barticks is used directly as a modulo divisor. It is 0 until an M:
    field is parsed, and notelen_notediv_to_ticks() can round a bar down to
    0 ticks for a large meter divisor, which is why three other call sites
    in this file carry an explicit "if (barticks == 0) barticks = 1". Here
    it divides by zero. abc_add_gchord() already returns early on this.
  * gsteps is strlen(h->drum)/2, so a one character drum string (reachable
    with "%%MIDI drum 1", a leading digit is accepted as if it were a note)
    makes it 0. The "if( ++g == gsteps ) g = 0" wrap then never fires, g
    grows without limit and h->drum[g*2] reads far past the 80 byte array.
  * A drum step with a zero length, or an even position holding a digit
    rather than 'd' or 'z', leaves rtime unchanged, so the loop spins
    forever.
 .
 Applies to 0.8.9.0 and to upstream git master, in either order with the
 CVE-2026-108577 patch.
